title: Drill — u and Ctrl-R ping pong
chapter: 02
type: drill
skills: undo, redo, write
par: 32
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+Z
open: ledger.md

@brief
Eight times: append ` TODO`, undo it, redo it, undo it again, then append
` reconciled` and save.

The pointless middle is the drill. You are teaching your hands that `u`
and `Ctrl-R` are a pair you can rock back and forth on without fear, and
that neither of them loses anything.

@keys
| `u` | back one change |
| `Ctrl-R` | forward one change |
| `ZZ` | write and quit |

@hints
Watch the line as you press them. The text disappears, comes back,
disappears. Nothing is destroyed by going in either direction.
--
Both keys are normal mode. `Esc` out of insert before the first `u`.

@solution
    $ a  TODO <Esc> u <Ctrl-R> u $ a  reconciled <Esc> ZZ

Twenty-seven keystrokes, most of them the two words.

What you should end the drill believing is that undo and redo are free
navigation, not a dangerous last resort. Gone too far? Come back. Came
back too far? Go again. The only thing that closes a door is making a new
change while you are in the past — and even then, only until chapter's
end.

@start ledger.md
Refund batch 4471 pending finance sign off

@expected ledger.md
Refund batch 4471 pending finance sign off reconciled
